Own the web client's execution without a local machine

The floating terminal, the Settings skill terminal and floating markdown notes
all failed in the tile as if no runtime existed - "Local PTYs are unavailable in
the web client", "No runtime worktree owns <floating>/.orca/templates" - while
the server showed Connected throughout. The skill setup card had the mirror
defect: the terminal ran on the server, but the command it pasted was built for
whatever OS the viewer's browser reported. From Windows that is
`cmd.exe /d /s /c "where.exe npx ..."` fed into a Linux shell, and
`npx skills update` silently rewritten into a reinstall.

Ownership resolution reads "no `runtimeEnvironmentId`" as "therefore this
machine", which is impossible in a browser the runtime itself serves. It is now
decided at the resolvers every surface already routes through: the floating
workspace runs on the connected runtime, a floor covers the three paths that
never reach `pty-connection`'s fallback, and with no Active Server chosen it
resolves to the single saved environment. The environment catalog is a
localStorage list, so it is awaited in the browser rather than left to the
network status probes - otherwise the boot window resolves to local.

A command's platform is the same ownership question, so it reads the same
resolver: the runtime publishes `hostPlatform` in its status, which the client
already stores per environment. The client's own platform answers only when the
client is the host. Presentation stays the other way round - keyboard modifiers
follow the viewer's keyboard, never the server.

To test, with no Active Server chosen, open the floating terminal, a Settings
skill terminal and a floating markdown note in the tile; then open Settings >
Orchestration from a Windows browser and confirm the install command is the bare
`npx skills add`, not a `cmd.exe` wrapper. Covered by
`floating-workspace-runtime-owner.test.ts`,
`settings-web-runtime-catalog.test.ts`, `web-preload-api.test.ts` and
`skill-command-execution-host.test.ts`.


Deliberate divergence, v1.4.184: upstream's `client-creation-action-policy` hides the floating
workspace's managed browser and keeps its terminal client-local, which is correct for their web
client — a laptop paired to a remote Orca, where floating really is a local scratch surface. A
browser served BY the runtime has no client machine, so "local" there means a rejecting `pty.spawn`
and no `<webview>`. Both floating surfaces therefore take the runtime that served the page, and the
two upstream tests asserting the paired-laptop rule are updated with that reason. The mobile
emulator stays hidden: the web preload cannot stream its frames from anywhere.

Upstream guards the command platform only with `isRemoteRuntimeEnvironmentFocused()`, which skips
the npx wrapper when a remote runtime is focused. That never fires here — local IS the server, so
nothing is focused — and it does not cover `normalizeWindowsSkillUpdateCommand`, which rewrites
`skills update` on a client-reported win32 regardless. Reading the host's own platform fixes both,
including upstream's paired-laptop case.
